## Why This Is Asked
This question assesses understanding of bridging, threading, backpressure management, and lifecycle handling in a practical logging scenario.

## Key Concepts
- React Native bridge patterns (DirectEvent vs EventEmitter)
- Background thread file tailing with rotation detection
- Fixed-size buffering and batch emission to preserve ordering guarantees
- Backpressure strategies: dropping oldest versus dropping newest
- App lifecycle handling for background task management

## Usage Snippet
```javascript
const Logs = require('react-native-logs');

const logs = Logs.startLogStream({
  logFilePath: '/var/log/app.log',
  pollIntervalMs: 500,
  maxEntriesPerBatch: 100,
});

logs.onLog((entries) => {
  console.log('New log entries:', entries);
});

// Later, when done:
logs.stopLogStream();
```

## Trade-offs
| Strategy | Trade-off |
|----------|------------|
| Drop oldest | Lower memory floor; risk losing historical context |
| Drop newest | Preserve context; may miss recent critical errors |
| File rotation by inode | Reliable on most filesystems; requires platform-specific handling |
| File rotation by size | Simpler to implement; less robust |
| Pause on background | Lower power usage; may miss logs during suspension |
| Background task | Continuous logging; higher battery consumption |

